# Test: is a microphone producing a signal at all?
# A manual test, and the instrument is your eyes on the Arduino IDE's Serial Plotter.
# Nothing moves and nothing lights up. The one thing that changes in the room is that
# every speaker goes quiet and stays quiet - what you are listening for is music from a
# phone held at one body's microphone, and the piece singing over it would drown the
# very reading being taken.
# Read "plotter setup", beside this scenario, before clipping anything on. One of the two
# ways of wiring it pulls something off J11, and there is an order to do that in.
# It never ends on its own - press stop when you are done.

0 Every speaker is silenced, in one command, before anything is read. If a body was singing it stops now. If the link cannot carry that command the run says so and stops rather than reading five modules in a room full of its own tones.
0 Nothing turns. No body moves, no light comes on, no light goes off. Whatever was lit stays lit, and the bar stays where it stood.
0.5 Twice a second, all five analyser modules are read in one sweep - they share a strobe, so the five readings are of the same instant rather than of five different ones - and the page redraws seven band values for each body.
0 Beside each body's live row there is a peak row, the highest each band has reached since the run started. It is what you compare one ear against another with, and `forget the peaks` throws it away when something has changed - a probe moved, a louder track.
0 The page names which bodies are in `params > audio > wired bodies`, and says outright which are not. An unwired analyser input is a floating pin, and a floating pin reads garbage rather than silence, so those rows are noise wearing the costume of a measurement.
0 Now the half the software cannot see. Play music into one body from a phone, 20 to 30 centimetres from its microphone, at a level you would talk over. Music with a beat, not a steady tone - a steady tone and a steady fault look identical on this plot.
0 On the plotter, `min` and `max` should open away from each other in time with the music and close again, with `mean` sitting still on the flat reference line at 256. That line is 1.25 volts, which is where the microphone module rests when it hears nothing.
0 In a quiet room, watch the band between `min` and `max` slowly widen rather than narrow. That is the gain control winding up towards its 40 dB strap because it has nothing to hear, and it is a sign of life rather than of noise.
0 Read the two halves together, because that is the whole point of the test. The plot moving and the bands moving means this microphone and its whole chain are fine, and the next question is `test audio bringup`.
0 The plot moving and the bands staying flat means the fault is between the microphone wire and the MSGEQ7 - its input network, its supply, or the strobe. Nothing that reads the analyser could have told you that, which is why this test exists.
0 Both flat means the microphone, its supply, or the wire back to the body. Section 6 of "plotter setup" has a row for each shape the trace can take, including the ones that look like a signal and are not.
0 Walk the five bodies by moving the probe from one J11 odd pin to the next. Ground goes on first and comes off last, every time, and never onto an even pin - those are the analyser outputs, which is the thing this test exists to stop trusting.
0 Nothing is written to a file. Half of this measurement is on a screen this process cannot see, and a record of the other half alone would be a record of the half that was never in doubt.
0 Nothing is put back when you stop, because nothing was changed - except that the speakers are left silent, which is where they should be. If you took the second route and flashed the plotter sketch onto the installation's own board, putting firmware 4 back is `drivers > arduino > flash firmware`, and that page then says in the board's own words which firmware is on it.
